## 11. Security Model & Threat Analysis

### 11.1 Threat Model

**Adversary Capabilities**:
- Global passive adversary (GPA) with network monitoring
- Resource-unbounded computational power (except breaking PQC)
- Ability to compromise up to f < n/3 validators
- Physical access to non-TEE hardware

**Assumptions**:
- At least one cryptographic primitive remains unbroken
- Honest majority (≥2/3 validators)
- Hardware attestation cannot be forged (for TEE-enabled deployments)

### 11.2 Attack Vectors & Mitigations

| Attack | Mitigation |
|--------|------------|
| **Cryptographic break** | Multi-primitive cascade; ≥1 must remain secure |
| **Side-channel leakage** | Constant-time implementation + decoy execution |
| **Implementation bug** | Dual-path verification + formal proofs |
| **Network partition** | Epoch-bound consensus; no coordination required |
| **Hardware compromise** | Attestation gating + absorbing halt |
| **Supply chain attack** | Reproducible builds + cross-ISA verification |
| **Quantum computer** | Post-quantum primitives (Dilithium, SPHINCS+, etc.) |

### 11.3 Security Guarantees

**Safety**:
- Lyapunov-bound preservation (mathematically proven)
- Absorbing halt on invariant violation
- No consensus fork (deterministic execution)

**Liveness**:
- Progress under honest majority (≥2/3 validators)
- Epoch completion within timing bounds
- Graceful degradation under primitive failure

**Privacy**:
- Information-theoretic metadata indistinguishability (PETN-CRCS)
- Side-channel resistance (constant-time + decoys)
- Offline synchronization (clone protocol)

