## 🔷 Final Security Composition Theorem

**Theorem (Multi-Layer Collision Resistance)**:
> Let `Derive(input, epoch, validators)` be the leaf index derivation function defined above. For any probabilistic polynomial-time adversary `A` with access to quantum oracles for all hash primitives, the probability that `A` outputs `input ≠ input'` such that:
> ```
> Derive(input, epoch, validators) = Derive(input', epoch, validators)
> ```
> is bounded by:
> ```
> ε_effective ≤ ε_IT_MAC + ε_cascade_composition × ε_epoch_chain × ε_pow
> ```
> where:
> - `ε_IT_MAC = 2^-128` (information-theoretic)
> - `ε_cascade_composition ≤ 2^-512` (computational, multi-primitive)
> - `ε_epoch_chain ≤ 2^(-512×t)` for epoch `t` (historical chaining)
> - `ε_pow ≤ 2^-difficulty` (search-space reduction)
>
> For realistic parameters (t≥100, difficulty≥32), `ε_effective ≤ 2^-128 + 2^-50000 ≈ 2^-128`.

**Corollary (Practical Impossibility)**:
> Even with a hypothetical quantum computer capable of Grover-speedup against all hash primitives, the effective security remains ≥128 bits due to the information-theoretic MAC layer. No feasible attack exists.

